//! Buses: the signals of a serial interface, joined as one, the roles of the parts that have
//! one, and the pull-up resistors that hold their lines.
//!
//! An interface states its lines and, for each role of the bus, which way each line goes
//! (`lines`, ADR 0064): the master's `mosi` is an `output` and the slave's an `input`, the
//! slave's `miso` is an `output` that its row must call `tri_state` when the part's datasheet
//! says it is high impedance while the part is not selected. A part states the role it plays
//! (`.spi.role &= SpiRole::Slave`); a part that states none is judged by the kinds in its pin
//! table alone. The kind of each row in a part's table is still what its datasheet says (ADR
//! 0016): the role judges the table and the nets, and invents no datasheet fact. Names are those
//! of the device that has the bus. Every bus but `Usb2` has `.levels`, the logic levels of its pins
//! (`std::logic::Levels`): a part states them for the interface, as it states those of a `Logic`.
//!
//! The lines of a bus are plain `Pin`s (ADR 0023; `lines` of ADR 0064 now says which way each
//! role drives them). `Logic` is not a bus member: it gives every signal a reference rail whose
//! pins `~` then joins, so two chips on separate supplies of the same voltage would have their
//! supply nets merged by a signal join. The levels of a bus are the `Levels` member, which `~`
//! does not join (ADR 0064). The faults of ADR 0016 covered the roles when the parts had pin
//! tables with `output` and `input` pins, as ADR 0023 verified and tested: a UART joined with
//! `~` is two outputs on a net (the board crosses `tx` and `rx` a pin at a time); an `input`
//! with nothing on its net is refused; two SPI masters, or two slaves with `miso`, on one bus
//! are two outputs on a net; an SPI master and slave, and a USB host and device, join with `~`
//! as named. Not covered, as ADR 0023 wrote it: a bus joined to a passive connector is not
//! checked at all, because a connector has no direction.

import std::logic::Levels
import std::parts::passive::Resistor
import std::sources::Rail

/// A UART: a transmit line `tx`, a receive line `rx`, and the flow-control lines `rts` and
/// `cts`, and `baud`, the symbol rate, in the unit of a frequency. The names are the device's
/// own: `tx` is the line that a device sends on, so the two ends of a link cross, and `~`
/// between two `Uart` interfaces joins one end's `tx` to the other's `rx` and its `rts` to the
/// other's `cts` (`crosses`). A board that joins pin by pin writes the crossing itself, and a
/// `tx` joined to a `tx` is refused. A device without flow control gives no pin to `rts` and
/// `cts` and leaves them alone. Both ends are the same, so a `Uart` has no role, and two ends
/// make a link.
interface Uart {
    .tx: Pin
    .rx: Pin
    .rts: Pin
    .cts: Pin
    .levels: Levels
    .baud: Frequency

    lines limit 2 {
        .tx  output crosses .rx
        .rx  input
        .rts output crosses .cts optional
        .cts input optional
    }
}

/// The role of a part on an SPI bus.
enum SpiRole {
    /// Drives `sclk` and `mosi`, and reads `miso`.
    Master,
    /// Reads `sclk` and `mosi`, and drives `miso` while it is selected.
    Slave,
}

/// The clock mode of an SPI bus: the idle level of `sclk` and the edge that data is sampled on.
enum SpiMode {
    /// Idle low, sampled on the rising edge.
    Mode0,
    /// Idle low, sampled on the falling edge.
    Mode1,
    /// Idle high, sampled on the falling edge.
    Mode2,
    /// Idle high, sampled on the rising edge.
    Mode3,
}

/// An SPI bus: the clock `sclk`, the data out of the master `mosi`, the data out of the slave
/// `miso`, and the select `cs`. Both ends call the lines the same, so the master and the
/// slave join with `~`. A part states its role (`.spi.role &= SpiRole::Slave`) and the checker
/// judges the table against it, the nets (a `mosi` is never on a `miso`; one `cs` of a slave on
/// one net), and the number of masters on a bus (one). `~` joins `cs` as it joins every line, so
/// a second slave on one master's interface joins its `sclk`, `mosi` and `miso` pin by pin and
/// takes a select of its own. A slave's `miso` that is shared with another slave must be
/// `tri_state` in its row. `frequency` is the clock: the board states the clock it will run
/// (`.cpu.spi.frequency &= 8MHz`), each part bounds it by its datasheet
/// (`.spi.frequency <= 10MHz`), and `~` between two interfaces equates it, so one value runs
/// along the bus and a part whose bound it breaks is named. `mode` is the clock mode: a part that
/// supports exactly one states it (`.spi.mode &= SpiMode::Mode0`), a part that supports two or more
/// states nothing and its datasheet says which in a comment; the value is equated along the bus, so
/// two parts that can only be in different modes are refused, and nothing that is fine is.
interface Spi {
    .role: SpiRole
    .sclk: Pin
    .mosi: Pin
    .miso: Pin
    .cs: Pin
    .levels: Levels
    .frequency: Frequency
    .mode: SpiMode

    lines SpiRole::Master limit 1 {
        .sclk output
        .mosi output
        .miso input optional
        .cs   output optional
    }
    lines SpiRole::Slave {
        .sclk input
        .mosi input
        .miso output required
        .cs   input single
    }
}

/// The role of a part on an I2C bus.
enum I2cRole {
    /// Drives `scl` and starts every transfer.
    Controller,
    /// Reads `scl` and answers when it is addressed.
    Target,
}

/// An I2C bus: the data line `sda` and the clock `scl`. Both are open-drain: they are pulled
/// to the supply by a resistor on the bus, one on each line, and no device drives them high.
/// `std::bus::I2cPullups` is that resistor pair. The checker refuses a line that has no
/// resistor to a rail's positive pin (ADR 0044). `frequency` is the clock of the bus, as for
/// `Spi`. A bus may have several controllers and targets, so no `limit` is stated, and the
/// address of a target is not judged.
///
/// `capacitance` is the capacitance of this part's own pin, each of `sda` and `scl`: the datasheet's
/// `Ci` (the I2C-bus specification, UM10204 Rev. 7.0, Table 10, allows at most 10 pF per pin; a
/// multiplexer or switch may exceed it, its datasheet says). It is `optional` (ADR 0074): a part
/// whose datasheet states none leaves it out, and the sum of the pins of its bus that the checker
/// makes (`I2cPullups.bus_capacitance`, ADR 0102) is then not made, and names the part. Like the levels it is the part's own, so `~`
/// between two interfaces does not equate it. It states what one pin adds and not the bus's: the
/// capacitance of the whole line is `I2cPullups.bus_capacitance`.
///
/// `sink_current` is the current this part's driver can sink while it holds a line at its `vol`
/// (`.levels.vol`): the datasheet's `IOL`, at the `VOL` the datasheet gives with it (UM10204
/// Rev. 7.0, Table 10: `VOL` 0.4 V at 3 mA, and `IOL` at least 3 mA, 20 mA in Fast-mode Plus; a
/// part's own table has its own figures). The level is the one `Levels` already has, so a part
/// states both: `.i2c.levels.vol &= 0.4V` and `.i2c.sink_current &= 3mA`. It is `optional`
/// (ADR 0074): a part whose datasheet states none leaves it out, and the bound of the pull-up
/// that the checker makes for each part (`I2cPullups`, ADR 0102) is then not made for it, and the
/// build names the part when another on the bus states one. Like the capacitance it is the part's own, so `~` does
/// not equate it between two interfaces.
interface I2c {
    .role: I2cRole
    .sda: Pin
    .scl: Pin
    .levels: Levels
    .frequency: Frequency
    .capacitance: Capacitance optional
    .sink_current: Current optional

    lines I2cRole::Controller {
        .sda bidirectional
        .scl bidirectional
    }
    lines I2cRole::Target {
        .sda bidirectional
        .scl input
    }
}

/// The role of a part on a serial-wire debug port.
enum SwdRole {
    /// The debug probe: drives the clock and the reset, and reads the trace.
    Probe,
    /// The part that is debugged.
    Target,
}

/// A serial-wire debug port: the data line `swdio`, the clock `swclk`, the trace output `swo`
/// and the reset `reset`. The target's `swo` is an output, its `swclk` an input and its `swdio`
/// a bidirectional pin; a probe's connector has passive pins. `frequency` is the clock of the
/// port, as for `Spi`.
interface Swd {
    .role: SwdRole
    .swdio: Pin
    .swclk: Pin
    .swo: Pin
    .reset: Pin
    .levels: Levels
    .frequency: Frequency

    lines SwdRole::Probe limit 1 {
        .swdio bidirectional
        .swclk output
        .swo   input optional
        .reset output optional
    }
    lines SwdRole::Target {
        .swdio bidirectional
        .swclk input
        .swo   output optional
        .reset input
    }
}

/// The role of a part on a USB 2.0 port.
enum UsbRole {
    /// Supplies the bus and enumerates the devices.
    Host,
    /// Is enumerated, and draws from the bus.
    Device,
    /// Passes the bus through: a receptacle or a plug is not an end of the bus, and the role is
    /// the one of the end behind it. It has no `lines`, so the part is not judged by a table,
    /// counts toward no limit and is never asked for another role.
    Passthrough,
}

/// A USB 2.0 port: the data pair `dp` and `dm` and the bus power `vbus`, whose `pos` is VBUS
/// and whose `neg` is the ground. The host and the device join with `~`: D+ to D+ and D- to
/// D-. `vbus.current` is negative at the host, which supplies, and positive at a device that
/// draws. A receptacle (a connector that the bus goes through, `std::parts::connector::UsbC2`)
/// is not an end of the bus and states `UsbRole::Passthrough`, so the rules judge the ends
/// behind it; a part that is a host or a device by what is plugged in (OTG) states none.
///
/// `vbus` is a `Rail` and not a pin, which is a deviation from "vbus, gnd" as two pins (ADR 0023).
/// A rail carries the voltage and the current, so the connector's `vbus.current` follows from the
/// loads on the net (ADR 0007), and a board bounds it with one line, `.usb.vbus.current >=
/// -500mA`. The device side must declare the rail, with its sensing pin an optional `input` and
/// its `neg` joined to its ground.
interface Usb2 {
    .role: UsbRole
    .dp: Pin
    .dm: Pin
    .vbus: Rail

    lines UsbRole::Host limit 1 {
        .dp bidirectional
        .dm bidirectional
    }
    lines UsbRole::Device limit 1 {
        .dp bidirectional
        .dm bidirectional
    }
}

/// A pull-up resistor: one resistor from the positive pin of `supply` to `line`, which holds
/// the line high while nothing drives it. The board states the resistance, as a value with its
/// tolerance (`.pullup.resistance &= 10kohm +/- 5%`), and the solver picks a resistor that stays
/// inside it; a board that does not gets an unconstrained pick finding for the resistor, with
/// the member named (ADR 0065). Use it on a reset, an enable, an open-drain output or any one
/// line; `I2cPullups` is the same circuit twice, for the two lines of an I2C bus.
///
/// The module states what it draws from `supply`, so the net it is on has every current it
/// needs (ADR 0026): a plain `Resistor` between a rail and a line leaves the current of the
/// resistor unknown, the rail's net has two unknowns, and every check on that net waits. The
/// line is held low, at worst, for the share `line_low` of the time, 0 to 1 (a board that knows
/// its line is low only part of the time narrows it: `.pullup.line_low &= 0..0.1`). While it
/// is low the resistor carries the supply voltage over its resistance (the lowest the line
/// goes is 0 V; the line gives no low level of a driver, so this is the worst case, and a
/// driver's real level draws a little less), and while the line is released it carries nothing.
/// So the resistor's current, and with it the draw on `supply`, is 0 up to
/// `supply.voltage / resistance`. A board that states a current on `supply` itself states a
/// budget (ADR 0038): the draw must be able to fit in it. Two `PullUp`s on one rail add.
///
/// That is the steady draw, with two assumptions that the module offers and a board may
/// replace (ADR 0070):
///
/// - `overdrive`, how far above `supply` a driver holds the line, at most. It is 0 V: the line
///   is never driven above the supply. A line a 5 V driver pulls above a 3.3 V supply pushes
///   current back into the rail, a negative draw of up to `overdrive / resistance`, which a
///   board that has such a driver states (`.pull.overdrive &= 0V..1.7V`); the draw then
///   reaches below 0 and a budget of draws only fails.
/// - `supply_top`, the voltage the draw is worked out at. It is `supply.voltage`, the steady
///   range, which is what a budget and a power rating read. A board that wants the draw at
///   the transient its rail can reach states it (`.pull.supply_top &= .rail.peak`).
///
/// Why it exists (ADR 0070). A resistor from a rail's positive pin to one signal line, the
/// pull-up of a reset or an enable or an open-drain output, is a chain that starts on the rail's
/// net. A plain `Resistor` leaves its current unknown, so the net has two unknowns, the
/// resistor's through-current and the rail's current, the balance of the net cannot be solved
/// (ADR 0026), and every check that reads it waits: the regulator's output current and power, the
/// resistor's own power. The build lists them as not evaluated. ADR 0044 and `I2cPullups` solved
/// this for the two lines of an I2C bus; a pull-up on any other line had no module.
///
/// Where it lives. Next to `I2cPullups`, which has the same circuit twice. This file already
/// imports `Resistor` and `Rail`, and a pull-up is a circuit around a line.
/// `std/parts/passive.nsm` holds parts and imports nothing: a module there would make it import the
/// rails and would put a circuit with a rail among two-terminal parts. The module regulators,
/// `BuckStage` and `FeedbackDivider`, sit beside the parts they are built from. The cost is that a
/// reader looking for a pull-up looks in `bus`, which this comment and the guide answer.
///
/// Why `I2cPullups` is left alone. It was to be written with two `PullUp`s only if every existing
/// test, example and guide block stayed the same, with the same picks, trials and messages. It
/// does not. Tried with the two as `.sda_pullup` and `.scl_pullup`, `examples/enclosure_sensor`
/// built byte for byte as before, but five tests failed: the resistor is now at
/// `pullups.sda_pullup.resistor`, so tests that read `pullups.sda_pullup.current` and
/// `.resistance` fail, the list of picked parts by path changes, and a current budget stated on
/// `pullups.supply.current` stays at the stated `0A..2mA` where the sum of the two draws (1.4 to
/// 1.6 mA) was expected: through two inner modules the budget is not narrowed to what the circuit
/// draws, a difference in behaviour and not only in paths. A member's path is what a board and a
/// test name. The first try also had `.resistance == .sda_pullup.resistance`, the direction
/// `I2cPullups` states it in; with the two `PullUp`s the resistor inside stays unset, because the
/// solver evaluates a relation with a field alone on its left from the right side only, and the
/// wrapper's value is on the left (the pick was 330 ohm and the build was refused). So
/// `I2cPullups` keeps its own text. The two circuits repeat, and one change to the draw model needs
/// two edits: that is the cost.
///
/// What was not taken:
///
/// - The draw as a `?=` default on the supply: a default is the module's offer, and a draw is a
///   fact about the circuit.
/// - A pull-up as a `generic` that the solver picks a provider for: there is one circuit, and
///   nothing to pick between.
/// - Reading `supply.peak` in the equation. A relation that reads an unset value waits and is
///   listed as not evaluated, so every pull-up on a rail with no stated peak would print a note
///   that it did not print before, and its resistor's current would be unknown (the fault this
///   module fixes). A default of the rail's peak has the same effect. There is no way in the
///   language to read a value only when it is stated, and adding one is the owner's decision.
/// - Widening the lower end always, to `-(peak / resistance)`: it makes every budget on a pull-up's
///   rail fail or lose its meaning for a board with no such driver.
/// - Only writing both assumptions in the doc: a board that has the driver then has nothing to
///   state.
///
/// `.line_low` is the share of the time the line is held low. The time a line is driven above the
/// supply is not a share the module models: `.overdrive` is the worst case the line reaches, and
/// the draw's range is the span from it to the draw with the line low, so the two cannot be told
/// apart in time (the interval is wider than a mix of the two would give, never narrower).
///
/// Left open, for the owner (the module and the marker `required` on its resistance were decided
/// in principle on 2026-11-06; the rest was an agent's, told to pick an option and go on):
///
/// - The names `.line`, `.line_low` and `.resistor`.
/// - That the draw is the worst case, 0 to `supply.voltage / resistance`, unless the board
///   narrows `.line_low`.
/// - That the module is in `std/bus.nsm` and not in `std/parts/passive.nsm`.
/// - That `I2cPullups` is not re-expressed, for the reason of the paths.
/// - The two assumptions of the draw, `.overdrive` and `.supply_top`, which the owner asked to be
///   modelled on 2026-11-06 and whose names and defaults an agent chose.
module PullUp {
    .supply: Rail
    .line: Pin
    .resistance: Resistance
    .resistor: Resistor
    .resistance == .resistor.resistance
    .supply.pos ~> .resistor ~> .line

    .line_low: Number
    .line_low &= 0..1
    .overdrive: Voltage
    .overdrive ?= 0V
    .supply_top: Voltage
    .supply_top ?= .supply.voltage
    .resistor.current == (.line_low * .supply_top - .overdrive) / .resistor.resistance
}

/// The two pull-up resistors of an I2C bus, one on `sda` and one on `scl`, to the positive
/// pin of `supply`. The board states the resistance, as a value with its tolerance
/// (`.pullups.resistance &= 4.7kohm +/- 5%`), and the solver picks resistors that stay inside
/// it. There is one pair for the whole bus, wherever it sits; two pairs on one bus are in
/// parallel and the board's concern.
///
/// The rise time of the lines is a closed-form check (`docs/research/closed-form-checks.md`,
/// check 5): `.rise_time` is `0.8473 * resistance * bus_capacitance`, the time an `RC` charge
/// takes from 30 % to 70 % of the supply, `ln(7/3) = 0.8473`, between which the I2C-bus
/// specification measures the rise time (UM10204 Rev. 7.0, section 7.1, which has the same
/// `0.8473 * RC`). Its Table 11 has the limits: `tr <= 1000ns` (Standard-mode), `300ns`
/// (Fast-mode), `120ns` (Fast-mode Plus), and `Cb <= 400pF` (550 pF in Fast-mode Plus). A board
/// states the limit of the mode it runs, `.pullups.rise_time <= 300ns` and
/// `.pullups.bus_capacitance <= 400pF`: the module builds none in, since the mode is the board's
/// choice and a mode table in `std::bus` is the owner's to decide. A bound holds in the worst
/// case, the highest resistance (its tolerance) and the highest capacitance; the supply does not
/// enter, since both points are fractions of it.
///
/// `bus_capacitance` is the capacitance of one line, `sda` or `scl`: the pins of the parts
/// (`I2c.capacitance` of each, from the datasheets) and the traces, connector and cable, which no
/// part states and the board estimates as `trace_capacitance`. The checker adds them (ADR 0102):
/// the interfaces that share a net with `bus` are the parts on the bus, and `bus_capacitance` is
/// the sum of their `.capacitance` and `.trace_capacitance`. The board states only its estimate,
/// `.pullups.trace_capacitance &= 20pF..40pF`. A part that states no `.capacitance` leaves the sum
/// unstated, and when a bound waits for it the build names the part. With no estimate of the
/// traces nothing is derived, since the pins alone would pass a bound the traces break. A board
/// that states `bus_capacitance` itself is judged on its figure and the sum is not made for it.
/// `bus_capacitance`, `trace_capacitance` and the pins' are `optional` (ADR 0074): a board that
/// states none has no rise time, and a board that bounds the rise time with no capacitance is told
/// the check was not made.
///
/// What it assumes: one pull-up resistor of `resistance` on the line charges it and nothing else
/// (two pairs on a bus are in parallel, and the board states the parallel value; a current-source
/// pull-up or a bus buffer is not an `RC`); the driver releases the line and no device holds it;
/// the two points are 30 % and 70 % of the supply, as the specification measures, whatever the
/// levels a part states. Optimistic when the capacitance is underestimated, which is usual:
/// traces, connectors and cable are the large part, and a part's pin capacitance is a maximum
/// that its datasheet gives under its own conditions.
///
/// The other bound is from below (`docs/research/closed-form-checks.md`, check 5): the weakest
/// driver on the bus must be able to hold a line low against the pull-up, so `.resistance >=
/// (supply.voltage - low_level) / sink_current` (UM10204 Rev. 7.0, section 7.1, with its Table 10:
/// `VOL` 0.4 V at 3 mA, 20 mA in Fast-mode Plus). The checker states it for each part on the bus
/// (ADR 0102), with the part's own `.i2c.sink_current` and `.i2c.levels.vol`: one relation for each
/// interface that shares a net with `bus`, so the board states nothing and the message names the
/// part. Each part has its own pair because no single pair of the bus's figures is the worst case
/// (the least current and the greatest level can be two parts; `min` cannot give a value, ADR 0098).
/// The bound holds in the worst case: the supply at its greatest, each part's figures at the end
/// that asks for the most and the resistance at its low end. A part that states no `.sink_current`
/// has no relation, and when another part on the bus states one the build names it. A bus on which
/// no part states one has no bound, as before. A board may state `.sink_current` and `.low_level`
/// itself (`.pullups.sink_current &= .sensor.i2c.sink_current`, `.pullups.low_level &=
/// .sensor.i2c.levels.vol`): it is then judged on its figures, as before, and the parts' relations
/// are not made for it. `sink_current` is `optional` (ADR 0074): a board that states it with a
/// figure that has no value, or with no `low_level`, is told the check was not made, never a pass.
/// No figure is built in. It assumes one driver pulls the line and the pull-up is the only load
/// that sinks: a current-source pull-up, a bus buffer, two pairs in parallel (the board states
/// the parallel value) and devices that leak into the line are not in it.
///
/// The module states what it draws from `supply`, so the net it is on has every current it
/// needs (ADR 0026): a pull-up carries nothing while its line is released or its driver is off,
/// and at most the supply voltage over its resistance while the line is held low (the lowest the
/// line goes is 0 V; the bus interface gives no low level of a driver, so this is the worst case,
/// and a driver's real level draws a little less). `sda_low` and `scl_low` are the share of that
/// worst case, 0 to 1 each, and the two lines can be low at once. `supply.current` is the sum of
/// the two, worked out by the solver from the resistors the board's `.resistance` picks. A board
/// that states a current on `supply` itself states a budget (ADR 0038): the sum must be able to
/// fit in it. It is the steady draw, with the two assumptions of `PullUp`, offered the same
/// way: `overdrive` (0 V: no driver holds a line above `supply`; a negative draw of up to
/// `overdrive / resistance` otherwise) and `supply_top` (`supply.voltage`; the transient the
/// board states, for the draw at it). Both apply to both lines.
///
/// It is the model of `PullUp` twice, and is not written with two `PullUp`s, for the reason of the
/// paths given there. There is no `PullUp` module in ADR 0023, which said a single pull-up needs
/// nothing because a `Resistor` is already in `~>` chains and a module cannot be in a chain; ADR
/// 0070 added one, for the draw.
module I2cPullups {
    .bus: I2c
    .supply: Rail
    .resistance: Resistance
    .sda_pullup: Resistor
    .scl_pullup: Resistor
    .resistance == .sda_pullup.resistance
    .resistance == .scl_pullup.resistance
    .supply.pos ~> .sda_pullup ~> .bus.sda
    .supply.pos ~> .scl_pullup ~> .bus.scl

    .sda_low: Number
    .scl_low: Number
    .sda_low &= 0..1
    .scl_low &= 0..1
    .overdrive: Voltage
    .overdrive ?= 0V
    .supply_top: Voltage
    .supply_top ?= .supply.voltage
    .bus_capacitance: Capacitance optional
    .trace_capacitance: Capacitance optional
    .rise_time: Time
    .rise_time == 0.8473 * .resistance * .bus_capacitance
    .sink_current: Current optional
    .low_level: Voltage
    .resistance >= (.supply.voltage - .low_level) / .sink_current
    .sda_pullup.current == (.sda_low * .supply_top - .overdrive) / .sda_pullup.resistance
    .scl_pullup.current == (.scl_low * .supply_top - .overdrive) / .scl_pullup.resistance
}

/// A pull-down resistor: one resistor from `line` to the negative pin of `reference`, which
/// holds the line low while nothing drives it. The board states the resistance, as a value with
/// its tolerance (`.pulldown.resistance &= 100kohm +/- 5%`), and the solver picks a resistor
/// that stays inside it; a board that does not gets an unconstrained pick finding for the
/// resistor, with the member named (ADR 0065). Use it on a boot-mode pin, an enable, a gate or
/// any one line that must read low when its driver lets go; `PullUp` is the same circuit to the
/// other rail.
///
/// The module states what its resistor carries, so no check on it waits for a current that
/// nothing gives (ADR 0026): a plain `Resistor` between a line and a ground leaves its current
/// unknown. The line is high, at worst, for the share `line_high` of the time, 0 to 1 (a board
/// that knows its line is high only part of the time narrows it:
/// `.pulldown.line_high &= 0..0.1`). While it is high the resistor carries the voltage of the
/// line over its resistance, and while the line is low or released it carries nothing. So the
/// resistor's current is 0 up to `line_top / resistance`, the current a driver puts into it.
///
/// Unlike `PullUp` the module draws nothing from any rail: the current comes from the driver of
/// the line, whose own supply is not this module's. `reference` is the rail that names the
/// ground of the resistor and the level the line is held against, a reference for the checker and
/// not a load, so the module states `.reference.current &= 0A`, as `Levels` does, and a board
/// that joins it to a supply (`.pulldown.reference ~ .supply`) adds no unknown to the net.
///
/// That is the steady current, with one assumption that the module offers and a board may
/// replace (ADR 0070):
///
/// - `line_top`, the highest voltage the line is held at, relative to the negative pin of
///   `reference`. It is `reference.voltage`, the steady range: the line is never driven above
///   the rail it is judged against. A line that a 5 V driver holds above a 3.3 V reference is
///   stated by a board that has such a driver (`.pulldown.line_top &= 0V..5.25V`), and the
///   current then reaches `line_top / resistance`.
///
/// Why it exists (ADR 0079). A resistor from a logic line to ground, the pull-down of a boot-mode
/// pin, an enable or a gate, is a chain that starts on a signal pin. A plain `Resistor` leaves its
/// through-current unknown: the net of the line is not balanced (a balance exists only for a net a
/// rail draws from or returns to, ADR 0007, ADR 0026), so nothing sets it. The drop and the power
/// of the resistor then wait, and the report has a note for each. The Ohm bound (the amendment of
/// ADR 0026) proves the power of a resistor whose two ends have known voltage ranges and sets no
/// current, so every other check that reads the current still waits, and the track width of the
/// net stays a lower bound. It mirrors `PullUp` (ADR 0070).
///
/// The module draws nothing from a rail, because the current comes from the line's driver, whose
/// supply is not the module's. `.reference` is therefore a reference and not a load, as the
/// `.reference` of `Levels` is, and states `.reference.current &= 0A` so that joining it to the
/// board's rail leaves no unknown on that net (ADR 0066, ADR 0077).
///
/// What was not taken:
///
/// - Naming the rail `.supply` and stating a draw on it, as `PullUp` does. A pull-down returns into
///   the rail's `neg` what the driver sent and takes nothing from `pos`; a stated draw would be a
///   lie that makes the net's current wrong.
/// - A pair, `.line_low` and `.line_high`. One share is enough: the resistor carries current only
///   while the line is high.
/// - A negative current for a line driven below ground (the `overdrive` of `PullUp`). No driver in
///   the cases at hand does it, and a board can state `.line_top` with a negative end for the
///   current the other way. Add it when a board needs it.
/// - A bare range `0..line_top / resistance` without the share. The share is what `PullUp` offers a
///   board that knows its line is low only part of the time, and a board that knows its line is
///   high only part of the time has the same need.
///
/// What it does not do: it does not give the driver's output current, nor the net's balance, so a
/// check that needs the driver's current waits as before. It states no input current for the pins
/// on the line. An optional `.input_current` on `Logic` was asked for (issue 192); it cannot join a
/// balance that does not exist, a leakage as a current source was refused in ADR 0076 section 8,
/// and owner decision 2 of ADR 0076 asks for the datasheet's own form of `Ii`, shown to the owner
/// first. (`Logic` has since an optional `.ii`, ADR 0076 amendment 8, which ADR 0079 does not
/// mention.)
///
/// Left open, for the owner (a std module in today's language, no new syntax): the names
/// `reference`, `line_high` and `line_top`, and the choice that the module draws nothing from a
/// rail, were made by an agent; the pin's input current was not built and is left to the owner.
/// That the share is of the line being high, and that `.line_top` is the board's to widen for a
/// stronger driver, are the other things to look at.
module PullDown {
    .reference: Rail
    .reference.current &= 0A
    .line: Pin
    .resistance: Resistance
    .resistor: Resistor
    .resistance == .resistor.resistance
    .line ~> .resistor ~> .reference.neg

    .line_high: Number
    .line_high &= 0..1
    .line_top: Voltage
    .line_top ?= .reference.voltage
    .resistor.current == .line_high * .line_top / .resistor.resistance
}
